## Debug Printing Structs

When debugging, you need to inspect struct values. Without a display implementation, printing a struct directly doesn't work. Use `derive[Debug]` to get automatic debug formatting.

### Without Debug

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Int; y: Int; }

let p = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
// io.println(p);  // ERROR: cannot print Point
```

### With Debug

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Int; y: Int; } derive[Debug]

let p = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
io.debug(p);  // prints: Point{ x: 1, y: 2 }
```

`io.debug()` outputs the struct name and all field names with their values.

### Debug Format

The output format is `TypeName{ field: value, ... }` — matching the syntax you'd use to create the struct. This makes it easy to copy-paste values during debugging.

```xiom
pub type User = {
  name: Str;
  email: Str;
  active: Bool;
} derive[Debug]

let u = User{ name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com", active: true };
io.debug(u);
// User{ name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com", active: true }
```

### Nested Structs

Inner structs must also implement Debug:

```xiom
pub type Address = { city: Str; zip: Int; } derive[Debug]
pub type Person = { name: Str; address: Address; } derive[Debug]

let p = Person{
  name: "Bob",
  address: Address{ city: "Rome", zip: 100 }
};
io.debug(p);
// Person{ name: "Bob", address: Address{ city: "Rome", zip: 100 } }
```

### Debug vs Display

- **Debug**: for developers, shows internal structure — `io.debug(value)`
- **Display**: for users, shows formatted output — needs manual implementation

